tool_args.new_string:
\paragraph{Depth-graduated curation and deployment.}
Predicted depth can serve as a quality proxy for importance weighting \cite{dsir2023,qurating2024}, down-weighting deeply recursive samples that binary filters \cite{drayson2025_detection_prevents_collapse} would indiscriminately discard.
Per-strategy normalization partially corrects the temperature-induced d1/d2 inversion (\S\ref{sec:gen:crossstrategy}) but leaves a residual gap, motivating a two-stage pipeline of strategy identification followed by strategy-conditioned classification.

\paragraph{Mixed-depth corpora.}
Real-world documents may interleave segments at different recursion depths; passage-level classification would assign a single label, potentially producing misleading estimates.
Extending the framework to segment-level depth estimation is a natural next step, though it introduces challenges in boundary detection and short-segment feature reliability.

\paragraph{Broader outlook.}
Adversarial paraphrasing \cite{sadasivan2023_paraphrase} could disrupt the statistical fingerprints we exploit; combining depth estimation with watermarking \cite{kirchenbauer2023_watermark} may provide more robust provenance tracking.
More broadly, depth estimation complements model attribution \cite{uchendu2020_attribution} and watermarking in a unified data provenance toolkit.
Scaling to larger models (7B+) and deeper recursion ($d{>}5$) may reveal different collapse dynamics and require more efficient feature computation.

